🚀 Merge Strings Alternately | Clean & Simple String Traversal

💡 Intuition
The idea is simple: take one character from word1, then one from word2, and keep alternating. 🔄

If one string finishes before the other, simply append the remaining characters from the longer string.

For example:

abc + pqr → a p b q c r → apbqcr ✨

🛠️ Approach
Maintain two pointers, i for word1 and j for word2.
Add a character from word1 whenever i is valid.
Add a character from word2 whenever j is valid.
Continue until both strings are completely processed.
The use of || in the loop is important because one string may be longer than the other. ✅

⏱️ Complexity
Time Complexity: O(n + m)
Space Complexity: O(n + m) for the resulting string
💻 Code
class Solution {
public:
    string mergeAlternately(string word1, string word2) {
        string ans = "";
        int i = 0, j = 0;

        while (i < word1.size() || j < word2.size()) {

            if (i < word1.size()) {
                ans += word1[i];
                i++;
            }

            if (j < word2.size()) {
                ans += word2[j];
                j++;
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }
};
✨ Key takeaway: Two-pointer traversal is a simple but powerful pattern for handling multiple sequences efficiently.

